INT. FLOODED GRAIN SILO - NIGHT

Black water at chest height. A single headlamp beam cuts the dust.

DMITRI VOLK, 40s, stocky, clings to a ladder rung. He keeps the lamp.

KOFI ASANTE, 30s, lean, shivers beside him, one arm wrapped around a rusted pipe.

BRONWEN HALE, 50s, a farmer's hands, floats a plastic drum like a raft, its lid taped shut.

DMITRI
The water's still coming up.

KOFI
It came up two rungs since the lamp was on my side.

BRONWEN
Then we climb.

DMITRI
He said the hatch is sealed from outside.

KOFI
Who did?

DMITRI
You. Ten minutes ago.

KOFI
I said I couldn't see the hatch. He was the one who saw it.

DMITRI
Which he?

KOFI
The man on the radio. Before it died.

BRONWEN
The radio man said the roof hatch opens inward. He said push, not pull.

DMITRI
He also said an hour. That was three hours ago.

KOFI
He didn't say that. He said maybe an hour. He was guessing.

BRONWEN
Both of you stop. Save the air.

DMITRI
There's no air shortage. There's a hatch shortage.

KOFI
Then give me the lamp and let me find it.

DMITRI
You'll drop it.

KOFI
I have not dropped a thing in my life.

BRONWEN
You dropped the radio.

KOFI
He dropped the radio. I only caught it badly.

DMITRI
Fine. Take the lamp. But if he asks, I didn't hand it over.

BRONWEN
Who is going to ask?

DMITRI
Whoever finds us.

Water laps another rung.

KOFI
Then we'd better be easy to find.
